Well, loosely speaking, an AI model

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.720
is an algorithm that uses training data to recognize

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:14.920
patterns and make predictions or decisions.

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:17.240
Now, open AI has got various models

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:19.200
geared towards different tasks.

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:22.600
And some models are newer and therefore better than others.

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:25.000
At the moment, there are two main categories of models

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:26.480
that you might come across.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:30.720
There's the GPT-3, GPT-3.5, and GPT-4 models.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:33.720
And GPT-4 is actually just coming out right now.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:35.400
It's currently in beta.

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:38.120
And these models are all about understanding and generating

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:39.360
natural language.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:42.680
And they can also generate computer languages as well.

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.800
Now, there's also the Codex models.

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:46.800
These models are specifically designed

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:49.000
to generate computer code, including

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.400
translating natural language to computer code and vice versa.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:55.080
And you've probably seen examples of that online,

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:57.440
even if you haven't tried it yet yourself.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:00.640
Open AI also has a model that filters content

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:04.520
to remove or flag unsafe or sensitive text.

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:08.040
In this project, we'll be using the text DaVinci 003 model,

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:10.880
which is a GPT-3.5 model.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:12.440
Now, this is one of the newest models.

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:14.360
It can provide long text output.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:16.920
And it's great of following instructions.

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.200
There's GPT-4, which is fresh out.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:22.360
And we will be coming to that later in this course.

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:25.600
There's the text Curie 001 model.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:27.920
And this is a very capable model.

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:31.000
It's actually faster than text DaVinci 003.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.600
But it's not as good overall in terms

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.440
of the language it creates.

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:39.040
There's text Babbage 001, which is great for straightforward

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.840
tasks and is also very, very fast.

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:44.040
And then there's text Ada 001.

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:45.960
And that is fine for basic tasks.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:47.800
And it's fast and cheap.

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:50.040
Now, these models are in age order.

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.080
So text DaVinci 003 is the newest on this list.

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:56.080
Text Ada 001 is the oldest.

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:59.040
And the older the models get, the less complex they are.

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:00.000
So they run faster.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.960
And generally speaking, they're cheaper.

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:06.120
But you should check the open AI docs for the latest prices.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:08.200
Now, the Curie, Babbage, and Ada models

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:11.560
all provide shorter outputs than text DaVinci 003.

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:14.120
But they might well still be capable of some or even

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:16.720
all of the tasks you might want to do in a project.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:19.080
It depends what it is you want to achieve.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.640
But just remember, with the older models being cheaper,

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:24.240
that means you can scale apps without incurring too

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:25.120
many costs.

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:26.480
And because they're faster, you'll

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:29.640
experience less of the horrible laggy UX.

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:32.120
You can sometimes get when you're working with any API,

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:34.600
but particularly with artificial intelligence,

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:37.240
as it obviously has to do so much computation.

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:40.040
Now, so far, our app hasn't been too laggy.

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:43.160
We've just been generating quite short, quick completions.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:45.680
But as our requests get more complex,

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:48.680
we will see lag times increase.

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.800
And now, you might well be asking, what model

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:52.720
should I use in my project?

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:55.520
Well, open AI's advice is this.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:57.440
Start with the best available model

00:29:57.440 --> 00:30:00.480
and downgrade to save time and costs where possible.

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.200
So basically, get your app working

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:04.200
so you're happy with its performance,

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:06.440
and then experiment with cheaper models

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:09.680
to see if you can get the same level of results.

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:12.280
Now, as new models come out, prices will change.

00:30:12.280 --> 00:30:15.080
And probably, performance will increase on those new models,

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:17.720
so you will have to do some experimentation.

