00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:24.160
complete, where we got cut off, the finished reason

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:25.400
was length.

00:52:25.400 --> 00:52:28.120
So generally speaking, a finished reason of length

00:52:28.120 --> 00:52:29.320
is bad news.

00:52:29.320 --> 00:52:34.000
It means OpenAI has not given us everything it wanted to.

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:38.520
Now, also, at the end here, we see completion tokens 59

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:43.320
in the first call and completion tokens 16 in the second call.

00:52:43.320 --> 00:52:45.200
So something we're doing with tokens

00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:47.640
is affecting the length of the completion we get.

00:52:47.640 --> 00:52:49.400
Now, as you're a seasoned coder, you've

00:52:49.400 --> 00:52:51.360
probably grasped that we can control

00:52:51.360 --> 00:52:54.040
the length of our completion to some extent

00:52:54.040 --> 00:52:56.320
with this max tokens property.

00:52:56.320 --> 00:52:59.080
But before we start using max tokens all over the place,

00:52:59.080 --> 00:53:02.480
we should really understand what a token is in OpenAI

00:53:02.480 --> 00:53:05.600
and what this number 60 really means.

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:08.600
So in the next scrim, let's take a peek under the hood

00:53:08.600 --> 00:53:12.760
and take a dive into tokens and the max tokens property.

00:53:12.760 --> 00:53:14.640
When you're ready for that, move on.

00:53:14.640 --> 00:53:18.920
OK, let's talk about tokens and the max tokens property.

00:53:18.920 --> 00:53:24.360
So we know that when we add this max tokens of 60

00:53:24.360 --> 00:53:28.080
to our API request, we get plenty of text back.

00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:31.560
We also know that if we don't set max tokens,

00:53:31.560 --> 00:53:35.640
we get much less text back and it's actually not complete

00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:38.560
and therefore doesn't really make sense.

00:53:38.560 --> 00:53:40.360
And that's why we're doing this.

00:53:40.360 --> 00:53:44.800
It's not complete and therefore doesn't really make sense.

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:47.640
And that's what we can actually see right here.

00:53:47.640 --> 00:53:50.360
So what exactly is going on with tokens

00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:53.200
and what even are tokens?

00:53:53.200 --> 00:53:57.760
OpenAI breaks down chunks of text for processing.

00:53:57.760 --> 00:53:59.080
Now, I say text.

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:01.560
It depends on which model you're using and what you're doing.

00:54:01.560 --> 00:54:04.480
It could also be code that gets broken down into chunks.

00:54:04.480 --> 00:54:06.200
But we're working with text, so we

00:54:06.200 --> 00:54:09.480
need to think about tokens in the context of text.

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:13.080
I think that each word or each syllable would be a token.

00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:15.960
But it's actually not as simple as that.

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:20.880
Roughly speaking, a token is about 75% of a word.

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:24.720
So 100 tokens is about 75 words.

00:54:24.720 --> 00:54:28.960
So the 60 token limit that we put on this fetch request

00:54:28.960 --> 00:54:32.440
would bring us back a maximum of about 40 words.

00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:35.560
When we didn't use max tokens at all,

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:40.720
this one here actually defaulted to 16, which as we can see here

00:54:40.720 --> 00:54:42.920
is way too short for our needs.

00:54:42.920 --> 00:54:44.640
So that's an important lesson.

00:54:44.640 --> 00:54:46.320
If you don't allow enough tokens,

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:48.600
your completion will be cut short.

00:54:48.600 --> 00:54:52.360
So you'll actually get less text back from OpenAI.

00:54:52.360 --> 00:54:54.480
So now you might be thinking, well, OK,

00:54:54.480 --> 00:54:56.840
a token is a chunk of text.

00:54:56.840 --> 00:54:57.600
But so what?

00:54:57.600 --> 00:54:59.560
Why do I need to think about limiting it?

00:54:59.560 --> 00:55:02.240
And the best way to make sure that your max token setting

00:55:02.240 --> 00:55:05.120
isn't causing you problems is to check the object you

00:55:05.120 --> 00:55:06.680
get with your response.

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:11.560
If you see the finish reason of length, that is a bad sign.

00:55:11.560 --> 00:55:13.640
That means the text that you're getting back

00:55:13.640 --> 00:55:15.000
has been cut short.

00:55:15.000 --> 00:55:18.840
And if you see a finish reason of stop, that is a good sign.

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:22.360
That means that OpenAI actually got to the end of the process

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:23.880
and it's given you all of the text

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:25.400
that it wanted to give you.

00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:28.600
So you might be thinking, OK, so a token is a chunk of text.

00:55:28.600 --> 00:55:30.600
But why does that matter?

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:33.040
Well, there are some good reasons for knowing about tokens

00:55:33.040 --> 00:55:34.920
and being able to limit them.

00:55:34.920 --> 00:55:40.040
Each token incurs a charge and it takes time to process.

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:42.280
So that gives you an incentive.

00:55:42.280 --> 00:55:44.240
If you limit the number of tokens,

00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:48.000
you can keep costs down and keep performance up.

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:49.880
And that's really important, of course,

00:55:49.880 --> 00:55:51.320
for when you run out of free credit

00:55:51.320 --> 00:55:53.120
and if you're creating a production app,

00:55:53.120 --> 00:55:55.840
and that app scales to millions and millions of users.

00:55:55.840 --> 00:55:57.040
And why shouldn't it?

00:55:57.040 --> 00:56:00.080
Now, there's something really important about max tokens

00:56:00.080 --> 00:56:02.200
that we need to understand.

00:56:02.200 --> 00:56:08.120
Max tokens does not help us control how concise a text is.

00:56:08.120 --> 00:56:11.480
As we saw in our app, we get an incomplete response

00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:14.960
when the token count is low, not a more concise one.

00:56:14.960 --> 00:56:17.360
So as a tool to control how verbose

00:56:17.360 --> 00:56:22.040
or how expressive OpenAI is, max tokens is useless.

00:56:22.040 --> 00:56:24.400
And that begs the question, how should I use it?

00:56:24.400 --> 00:56:26.520
And the answer is, we should set it high enough

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:29.400
to allow a full response from OpenAI.

00:56:29.400 --> 00:56:32.000
So you might just have to do a little bit of experimentation

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:34.840
with that each time and just making sure the text

00:56:34.840 --> 00:56:38.920
that you get back from the API is not cut short.

00:56:38.920 --> 00:56:42.480
So how can we control the length of text we get from OpenAI?

00:56:42.480 --> 00:56:45.320
Well, we do that with prompt design.

00:56:45.320 --> 00:56:47.400
Good prompt design is everything.

00:56:47.400 --> 00:56:49.520
And good prompt design is the best way

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:52.280
to ensure that the text we get from OpenAI

00:56:52.280 --> 00:56:54.040
is the length we want.

00:56:54.040 --> 00:56:55.800
Now, I actually think that the text

00:56:55.800 --> 00:56:58.720
that we got back when we had max tokens set to 60

00:56:58.720 --> 00:57:00.760
was just a little bit too long.

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:03.320
So as we go through this project and we learn more

00:57:03.320 --> 00:57:05.560
about prompt design, we will come back

00:57:05.560 --> 00:57:07.800
and just do a little bit of refactoring here.

