I've already got some HTML and CSS prepared for this project.

02:09:49.500 --> 02:09:52.700
So let's take a look at that and then get this chatbot working.

02:09:56.360 --> 02:09:59.060
Let's take a look at the code we've already got.

02:09:59.060 --> 02:10:02.700
We've got a form component right here and a button,

02:10:02.700 --> 02:10:07.220
which inside a form component will act as a submit button.

02:10:07.220 --> 02:10:11.100
Now, this div up here, this is where the conversation unfolds.

02:10:11.100 --> 02:10:13.940
And in it, we've got this one hard coded message,

02:10:13.940 --> 02:10:15.180
how can I help you?

02:10:15.180 --> 02:10:19.060
And that is the message that we can see right here.

02:10:19.060 --> 02:10:23.400
Now, over in index.js, we've got exactly the same setup

02:10:23.400 --> 02:10:24.620
as before.

02:10:24.620 --> 02:10:28.580
These lines of code right here are the open AI setup,

02:10:28.580 --> 02:10:30.900
just like they were in the previous project.

02:10:30.900 --> 02:10:33.540
And remember, in terms of the API key

02:10:33.540 --> 02:10:36.020
that we're bringing in from this.m file,

02:10:36.020 --> 02:10:39.180
we will be looking at deploying projects with API keys hidden

02:10:39.180 --> 02:10:40.860
towards the end of the course.

02:10:40.860 --> 02:10:44.180
And always be sure not to expose your API key

02:10:44.180 --> 02:10:46.380
when you're deploying projects, sharing them,

02:10:46.380 --> 02:10:49.540
or publishing them to public repositories.

02:10:49.540 --> 02:10:54.460
Now here, we've taken control of the chatbot conversation div.

02:10:54.460 --> 02:10:57.100
And we can see that right here in the HTML.

02:10:57.100 --> 02:10:58.860
This is the ID right here.

02:10:58.860 --> 02:11:01.260
So it's referring to this div.

02:11:01.260 --> 02:11:04.340
And that is the div where the conversation takes place.

02:11:04.340 --> 02:11:06.460
Now, we've got that stored in this const.

02:11:06.460 --> 02:11:08.220
And I've declared that globally, as we'll

02:11:08.220 --> 02:11:11.340
need to use it in multiple functions.

02:11:11.340 --> 02:11:14.020
Now, moving on down, we've got this event listener.

02:11:14.020 --> 02:11:16.760
And it is listening out for any submit event.

02:11:16.760 --> 02:11:19.440
And that will be triggered by a user clicking on the button

02:11:19.440 --> 02:11:21.900
or pressing Enter to submit the form.

02:11:21.900 --> 02:11:25.260
When a submit event is detected, we build a new element

02:11:25.260 --> 02:11:26.480
with create element.

02:11:26.480 --> 02:11:29.020
We then add the necessary CSS classes

02:11:29.020 --> 02:11:32.540
and append it to the chatbot conversation div.

02:11:32.540 --> 02:11:36.620
Then we populate it with whatever the user has inputted.

02:11:36.620 --> 02:11:39.740
And at that point, we can go ahead and clear the input

02:11:39.740 --> 02:11:41.060
field.

02:11:41.060 --> 02:11:44.460
Now, this line of code right here just moves the dialogue

02:11:44.460 --> 02:11:47.620
down so the latest messages are always in view.

02:11:47.620 --> 02:11:49.700
Else, as the conversation grows, you'd

02:11:49.700 --> 02:11:51.660
keep having to scroll down manually.

02:11:51.660 --> 02:11:53.860
Now, this is quite a neat way of doing it.

02:11:53.860 --> 02:11:56.380
But you could also use scroll into view.

02:11:56.380 --> 02:11:59.820
But I found when you're running a mini browser in a big browser,

02:11:59.820 --> 02:12:01.380
that can sometimes fail.

02:12:01.380 --> 02:12:03.660
So I went for this approach.

02:12:03.660 --> 02:12:07.300
Finally, we have this render typewriter text function.

02:12:07.300 --> 02:12:10.100
As the name suggests, it will give a typewriter effect

02:12:10.100 --> 02:12:12.820
to the AI's text as it is rendered.

02:12:12.820 --> 02:12:15.660
Now, there are loads of ways to do typewriter text.

02:12:15.660 --> 02:12:17.580
Some you can do just with CSS.

02:12:17.580 --> 02:12:19.660
But I've gone for a JavaScript approach.

02:12:19.660 --> 02:12:22.140
And what happens in this function is, again, we

02:12:22.140 --> 02:12:23.540
create a new element.

02:12:23.540 --> 02:12:26.340
We add the necessary CSS classes.

02:12:26.340 --> 02:12:29.080
This one includes a blinking cursor class,

02:12:29.080 --> 02:12:31.580
which would just give a nice blinking cursor

02:12:31.580 --> 02:12:34.100
effect to the text as it's rendered.

02:12:34.100 --> 02:12:36.260
And in fact, you can see the CSS for that

02:12:36.260 --> 02:12:38.380
right at the end of the CSS file.

02:12:38.380 --> 02:12:41.060
We've got the animation right there.

02:12:41.060 --> 02:12:43.780
The speed at which each individual character is rendered

02:12:43.780 --> 02:12:46.000
is controlled by this set interval.

02:12:46.000 --> 02:12:50.060
And at the moment, I've got it set to 50 milliseconds.

02:12:50.060 --> 02:12:51.500
And you can, of course, change that

02:12:51.500 --> 02:12:54.100
if you would prefer a different speed.

02:12:54.100 --> 02:12:56.780
Now, we won't be working much with the rest of the CSS

02:12:56.780 --> 02:12:57.900
in this file.

02:12:57.900 --> 02:13:00.420
But do pause and check it out if you would like to.

02:13:00.420 --> 02:13:02.420
There's nothing particularly special going on.

02:13:02.420 --> 02:13:05.860
But we have got a bit of CSS grid up here

02:13:05.860 --> 02:13:08.100
where we deal with the chatbot header.

02:13:08.100 --> 02:13:12.100
So the CSS grid is just used for this layout up here.

02:13:12.100 --> 02:13:14.100
OK, so that is what we have so far.

02:13:14.100 --> 02:13:16.740
Let's move on and take an overview of how the AI is

02:13:16.740 --> 02:13:18.620
going to work in this project.

02:13:18.620 --> 02:13:21.260
It's a little bit different to what we've done so far.

02:13:21.260 --> 02:13:22.580
So let's look at that next.

02:13:26.580 --> 02:13:30.140
OK, let's take an overview of how the GPT-4 model works

02:13:30.140 --> 02:13:31.460
with chatbots.

02:13:31.460 --> 02:13:33.780
So we're going to use one function

02:13:33.780 --> 02:13:36.060
to make requests to the API.

02:13:36.060 --> 02:13:40.020
And in a conversation, we'll use that function multiple times.

02:13:40.020 --> 02:13:42.140
But we need to think about what we send in the prompt

02:13:42.140 --> 02:13:44.700
because there's actually a big problem for chatbots

02:13:44.700 --> 02:13:46.220
that we have to overcome.

02:13:46.220 --> 02:13:48.940
And I want to illustrate that for you right here.

02:13:48.940 --> 02:13:52.300
So what I've got here is a simple call to the API

02:13:52.300 --> 02:13:56.060
using the create completion endpoint and the text

02:13:56.060 --> 02:13:59.020
DaVinci 003 model, which is the model we

02:13:59.020 --> 02:14:00.540
used in the previous project.

02:14:00.540 --> 02:14:02.860
And I chose it here because it should look pretty familiar

02:14:02.860 --> 02:14:03.700
by now.

02:14:03.700 --> 02:14:05.540
And I can use it to illustrate the point

02:14:05.540 --> 02:14:07.140
that I want to make about chatbots

02:14:07.140 --> 02:14:10.260
without getting caught up in new syntax we haven't studied yet.

02:14:10.260 --> 02:14:12.660
Now, I'm going to come in here to this prompt.

02:14:12.660 --> 02:14:14.540
And I'm going to ask a question.

02:14:17.220 --> 02:14:21.700
Where were the 2001 Wimbledon tennis championships held?

02:14:21.700 --> 02:14:24.420
OK, let's call that function.

02:14:24.420 --> 02:14:27.980
And we'll hit Save and open up the console.

02:14:27.980 --> 02:14:29.220
And we get the answer.

02:14:29.220 --> 02:14:31.860
The 2001 Wimbledon tennis championships

02:14:31.860 --> 02:14:34.740
were held at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet

02:14:34.740 --> 02:14:37.500
Club in Wimbledon, London, England.

02:14:37.500 --> 02:14:39.260
OK, so it's a correct answer.

02:14:39.260 --> 02:14:43.500
Now let's ask it who won that year.

02:14:43.500 --> 02:14:45.180
And I'll hit Save.

02:14:45.180 --> 02:14:47.940
And it tells us the Philadelphia 76ers

02:14:47.940 --> 02:14:51.260
won the 1982 NBA championship.

02:14:51.260 --> 02:14:53.940
And this is what's called a hallucination.

02:14:53.940 --> 02:14:57.500
The AI makes up a linguistically plausible answer

02:14:57.500 --> 02:15:00.060
when it doesn't know the right answer.

02:15:00.060 --> 02:15:02.060
And we'll talk more about hallucinations

02:15:02.060 --> 02:15:03.780
later in this course.

02:15:03.780 --> 02:15:05.940
Now, it figures that it doesn't know the answer.

02:15:05.940 --> 02:15:08.500
The question who won it that year only

02:15:08.500 --> 02:15:11.740
makes sense in the context of the previous question, which

02:15:11.740 --> 02:15:14.980
had the keywords Wimbledon and 2001.

02:15:14.980 --> 02:15:18.180
And that exposes a big problem for chatbots,

02:15:18.180 --> 02:15:21.900
which is that models have no memory of past completions.

02:15:21.900 --> 02:15:24.460
And that means that all relevant information

02:15:24.460 --> 02:15:27.500
must be sent with each API request.

02:15:27.500 --> 02:15:30.060
So I need to refactor my second question

02:15:30.060 --> 02:15:32.060
to include all of the information

02:15:32.060 --> 02:15:34.460
that the model needs to know, i.e.

02:15:34.460 --> 02:15:38.820
who won Wimbledon in 2001.

02:15:38.820 --> 02:15:40.660
Let's run that.

02:15:40.660 --> 02:15:45.180
And it says Goran Ivanicevic won Wimbledon in 2001.

02:15:45.180 --> 02:15:47.300
And that is true, with the only problem

02:15:47.300 --> 02:15:49.180
being that it's assumed I was talking

02:15:49.180 --> 02:15:50.940
about the men's championships.

02:15:50.940 --> 02:15:53.380
The women's championships were won by Venus Williams

02:15:53.380 --> 02:15:56.140
that year, and she beat the Belgian Justine Ennan

02:15:56.140 --> 02:15:57.540
in three sets.

02:15:57.540 --> 02:16:00.940
So there's just a little example of the biases picked up

02:16:00.940 --> 02:16:05.740
by AI models as they're fed data from the open internet.

02:16:05.740 --> 02:16:07.500
OK, so when it comes to chatbots,

02:16:07.500 --> 02:16:09.780
in order for the model to interact properly so

02:16:09.780 --> 02:16:12.260
the conversation flows and remains logical,

02:16:12.260 --> 02:16:15.820
the model needs to know the context of the conversation.

02:16:15.820 --> 02:16:18.900
And we achieve that by sending the conversation as it exists

02:16:18.900 --> 02:16:21.740
so far with each request.

02:16:21.740 --> 02:16:25.460
So let's look at a diagram of how the main AI business logic

02:16:25.460 --> 02:16:28.740
is going to work with this chatbot.

02:16:28.740 --> 02:16:30.260
So the first thing that we'll need to do

02:16:30.260 --> 02:16:32.660
is store the conversation in an array.

02:16:32.660 --> 02:16:35.820
And that is what's represented here by this box.

02:16:35.820 --> 02:16:37.500
And this does have to be an array.

02:16:37.500 --> 02:16:40.780
The GPT-4 model and the endpoint we're going to be using

02:16:40.780 --> 02:16:42.580
have much stricter rules on syntax

02:16:42.580 --> 02:16:45.660
than the text DaVinci 003 model and the Create Chat

02:16:45.660 --> 02:16:47.260
completion endpoint.

02:16:47.260 --> 02:16:48.860
Now, once we've set up this array,

02:16:48.860 --> 02:16:51.420
we'll use some special OpenAI syntax

02:16:51.420 --> 02:16:54.660
to instruct the chatbot and tell it how we want it to behave.

02:16:54.660 --> 02:16:56.620
And then we'll store that instruction

02:16:56.620 --> 02:17:00.180
in an object at the first index of the array.

02:17:00.180 --> 02:17:02.740
And I've just put the curly braces either side

02:17:02.740 --> 02:17:04.940
of instructions just to make it clear

02:17:04.940 --> 02:17:10.100
that that will be an object and that this is an array of objects.

02:17:10.100 --> 02:17:11.980
So now we've got the instructions

02:17:11.980 --> 02:17:14.020
at the first index of the array.

02:17:14.020 --> 02:17:16.580
We will take some input from the user.

02:17:16.580 --> 02:17:18.420
And the first thing to do with that input

02:17:18.420 --> 02:17:20.140
is to render it to the DOM.

02:17:20.140 --> 02:17:23.460
Then we'll use some OpenAI syntax to format it.

02:17:23.460 --> 02:17:27.820
And again, we'll save it in an object in conversation array.

02:17:27.820 --> 02:17:30.580
Once that's done, we're going to send the entire conversation

02:17:30.580 --> 02:17:33.860
array off to the OpenAI API.

02:17:33.860 --> 02:17:36.580
And what we'll get back, of course, is the response.

02:17:36.580 --> 02:17:39.820
And what we'll need to do then is render the completion

02:17:39.820 --> 02:17:42.700
to the DOM and then place the completion in an object

02:17:42.700 --> 02:17:45.580
with the correct syntax that we'll be discussing shortly.

02:17:45.580 --> 02:17:49.100
And then we'll be adding it to conversation array.

02:17:49.100 --> 02:17:52.100
So right now, conversation array has got three objects.

02:17:52.100 --> 02:17:54.500
We've got the instructions, the user's input,

02:17:54.500 --> 02:17:56.700
and the first response from the API.

02:17:56.700 --> 02:17:59.540
And then as you can imagine, this process continues.

02:17:59.540 --> 02:18:01.100
We take the user's reply.

02:18:01.100 --> 02:18:02.620
We render it to the DOM.

02:18:02.620 --> 02:18:04.220
We place it in an object.

02:18:04.220 --> 02:18:06.740
We store that object in conversation array.

02:18:06.740 --> 02:18:08.900
And then we send it off to the API.

02:18:08.900 --> 02:18:10.300
We get back our response.

02:18:10.300 --> 02:18:13.420
And we render it to the DOM, place it in an object,

02:18:13.420 --> 02:18:16.500
and then store the object in conversation array.

02:18:16.500 --> 02:18:19.500
And that process continues with the user's response.

02:18:19.500 --> 02:18:21.660
And we just keep repeating as needed.

02:18:21.660 --> 02:18:25.260
And this can go on and on until you reach the token limit.

02:18:25.260 --> 02:18:27.860
But the token limit with the GPT-4 model

02:18:27.860 --> 02:18:29.580
is extremely generous.

02:18:29.580 --> 02:18:31.940
So it would have to be a very, very long conversation,

02:18:31.940 --> 02:18:34.620
indeed, to reach that limit.

02:18:34.620 --> 02:18:36.260
OK, that's the overview.

02:18:36.260 --> 02:18:37.220
Let's get to work.

02:18:40.780 --> 02:18:43.820
OK, so we need a place to store our conversation.

02:18:43.820 --> 02:18:46.780
This is going to be the single source of truth, as it were.

02:18:46.780 --> 02:18:49.620
The place where everything we ask of openAI

02:18:49.620 --> 02:18:52.260
and everything it replies to us is stored.

02:18:52.260 --> 02:18:53.660
So I'm going to come in here.

02:18:53.660 --> 02:18:57.580
And I'm going to set up a const called conversation array.

02:18:57.580 --> 02:19:00.900
And in fact, I'll abbreviate array to just R.

02:19:00.900 --> 02:19:03.260
And remember, this is going to store an array

02:19:03.260 --> 02:19:06.300
because the API needs to have this conversation

02:19:06.300 --> 02:19:09.020
as an array of objects.

02:19:09.020 --> 02:19:11.540
So let's look at the first object that we need.

02:19:11.540 --> 02:19:14.060
It's the object that holds the instruction.

02:19:14.060 --> 02:19:17.940
This is where we tell the chatbot how we want it to behave.

02:19:17.940 --> 02:19:22.260
Now, this instruction object consists of two key value pairs.

02:19:22.260 --> 02:19:25.740
And that will be the same for all of the objects in this array.

02:19:25.740 --> 02:19:27.860
They're going to follow a similar pattern.

02:19:27.860 --> 02:19:30.060
The first key will be role.

02:19:30.060 --> 02:19:32.900
And this should correspond to the value system.

02:19:32.900 --> 02:19:35.540
This is just telling openAI that what comes next

02:19:35.540 --> 02:19:39.100
is the instruction and not part of the conversation.

02:19:39.100 --> 02:19:40.820
The second key will be content.

02:19:40.820 --> 02:19:44.660
And this should correspond to a string with an instruction.

02:19:44.660 --> 02:19:46.300
And this is your chance to influence

02:19:46.300 --> 02:19:49.100
how the AI behaves and responds.

02:19:49.100 --> 02:19:50.860
So I'm going to come in here.

02:19:50.860 --> 02:19:53.900
And I'll set up the first object in this array.

02:19:53.900 --> 02:19:56.540
So the first key will be role.

02:19:56.540 --> 02:19:59.380
And we'll set that to the string system.

02:19:59.380 --> 02:20:02.020
And the second key will be content.

02:20:02.020 --> 02:20:03.980
And this will hold our instruction.

02:20:03.980 --> 02:20:07.860
And for now, I'm going to put something fairly mainstream.

02:20:07.860 --> 02:20:10.660
I've gone for you are a highly knowledgeable assistant.

02:20:10.660 --> 02:20:12.460
That is always happy to help.

02:20:12.460 --> 02:20:14.660
Now, later on in the project, when the chatbot

02:20:14.660 --> 02:20:16.460
is up and running, we will actually

02:20:16.460 --> 02:20:18.700
revisit this instruction and have some fun with it.

02:20:18.700 --> 02:20:21.380
But for now, let's keep things simple.

02:20:21.380 --> 02:20:23.780
OK, so we've got the conversation array

02:20:23.780 --> 02:20:25.900
and the instruction object set up.

02:20:25.900 --> 02:20:28.340
Now, let's deal with the user's inputted text,

02:20:28.340 --> 02:20:31.580
which we'll also be adding to this conversation array.

02:20:31.580 --> 02:20:33.540
So let's come on to that next.

02:20:37.220 --> 02:20:40.500
OK, so we have the instructions object in the array.

02:20:40.500 --> 02:20:43.980
And now we need to add the user's input.

02:20:43.980 --> 02:20:48.060
So in index.js, we've got this event listener listening out

02:20:48.060 --> 02:20:49.300
for a submit.

02:20:49.300 --> 02:20:52.980
And when it detects the submit, the anonymous function

02:20:52.980 --> 02:20:58.260
takes the user's input and saves it to this const user input.

02:20:58.260 --> 02:21:01.180
So what we need to do is take that incoming text

02:21:01.180 --> 02:21:05.140
and put it into an object and push it to conversation array.

02:21:05.140 --> 02:21:09.300
Now, we've already seen how the chatgpt model wants its objects.

02:21:09.300 --> 02:21:11.660
And we've got an example of it right here.

02:21:11.660 --> 02:21:15.660
We've got an object with two key value pairs, role and content.

02:21:15.660 --> 02:21:19.780
And what we're going to do next is actually very, very similar.

02:21:19.780 --> 02:21:23.100
The object is going to have two key value pairs.

02:21:23.100 --> 02:21:25.540
The first key will be role.

02:21:25.540 --> 02:21:28.540
And this should correspond to the value user.

02:21:28.540 --> 02:21:30.580
The second key will be content.

02:21:30.580 --> 02:21:33.820
And this should correspond to a string holding whatever

02:21:33.820 --> 02:21:35.780
the user has inputted.

02:21:35.780 --> 02:21:39.740
So right here inside the event listener's anonymous function,

02:21:39.740 --> 02:21:41.700
I have written you a challenge.

02:21:41.700 --> 02:21:44.660
I want you to push an object holding the user's input

02:21:44.660 --> 02:21:46.460
to conversation array.

02:21:46.460 --> 02:21:48.660
And then just log out conversation array

02:21:48.660 --> 02:21:50.220
to check it's worked.

02:21:50.220 --> 02:21:52.380
Now, I'm going to leave this slide right here

02:21:52.380 --> 02:21:55.460
so you know exactly what that object should hold.

02:21:55.460 --> 02:21:58.780
OK, pause now, get that sorted, and I'll see you back here

02:21:58.780 --> 02:21:59.660
in just a moment.

02:21:59.660 --> 02:22:03.180
OK, so hopefully, you managed to do that just fine.

02:22:03.180 --> 02:22:06.100
So I'm going to come in here, and I'm

02:22:06.100 --> 02:22:12.380
going to say conversation array, and I'll use.push.

02:22:12.380 --> 02:22:14.540
And we're going to push an object.

02:22:14.540 --> 02:22:17.540
And we know the first key will be role.

02:22:17.540 --> 02:22:20.660
And that will have the value of the string user.

02:22:20.660 --> 02:22:23.100
The second key will be content.

02:22:23.100 --> 02:22:25.940
And that will hold whatever object

02:22:25.940 --> 02:22:29.900
the user has inputted. OK, now let's log out conversation

02:22:29.900 --> 02:22:32.820
array and see if it's worked.

02:22:32.820 --> 02:22:36.940
So I'll open up the console, and I'll ask a question.

02:22:36.940 --> 02:22:37.700
And there we are.

02:22:37.700 --> 02:22:40.500
We are logging out conversation array.

02:22:40.500 --> 02:22:43.220
And you can see that we've got two objects.

02:22:43.220 --> 02:22:45.220
