02:33:45.180 --> 02:33:48.620
So let's take a look at how we can deal with that next.

02:33:53.980 --> 02:33:56.260
At the moment, we're only using two properties

02:33:56.260 --> 02:33:58.460
when we make a request to the API.

02:33:58.460 --> 02:34:01.200
We're using the model and the messages.

02:34:01.200 --> 02:34:03.040
And they are both required.

02:34:03.040 --> 02:34:06.420
Now, in the previous project, we used max tokens, which,

02:34:06.420 --> 02:34:08.580
as I said, is not so helpful here.

02:34:08.580 --> 02:34:11.380
And we also use temperature, which you're welcome to add

02:34:11.380 --> 02:34:14.220
if you think your chatbot is not performing optimally.

02:34:14.220 --> 02:34:17.180
You can just go ahead and add it right here.

02:34:17.180 --> 02:34:20.580
And just like with the DaVinci model, it defaults to one.

02:34:20.580 --> 02:34:23.180
Now, I don't think we need to change the temperature on this,

02:34:23.180 --> 02:34:25.860
so I'm actually going to delete that.

02:34:25.860 --> 02:34:27.500
What I do want to look at, though,

02:34:27.500 --> 02:34:31.140
is two settings, frequency penalty and presence penalty.

02:34:31.140 --> 02:34:33.260
And what they do is offer some control

02:34:33.260 --> 02:34:35.700
over how repetitive our output is,

02:34:35.700 --> 02:34:38.380
because we want the language to sound natural,

02:34:38.380 --> 02:34:40.540
and we don't want to find ourselves saying,

02:34:40.540 --> 02:34:42.700
can you stop saying that?

02:34:42.700 --> 02:34:44.900
Now, we're going to look at these two settings together

02:34:44.900 --> 02:34:47.980
as they are similar and they're easy to confuse.

02:34:47.980 --> 02:34:50.300
So let's take presence penalty first.

02:34:50.300 --> 02:34:53.760
Presence penalty will be a number from minus two to two,

02:34:53.760 --> 02:34:57.400
and you can change it in increments of 0.01.

02:34:57.400 --> 02:35:00.740
It defaults to zero, so that is what we're using now

02:35:00.740 --> 02:35:02.860
because it's unset.

02:35:02.860 --> 02:35:05.460
At higher numbers, presence penalty increases

02:35:05.460 --> 02:35:08.820
the model's likelihood of talking about new topics.

02:35:08.820 --> 02:35:10.660
So what does that mean in the real world?

02:35:10.660 --> 02:35:14.020
Well, let's imagine a conversation between two friends,

02:35:14.020 --> 02:35:16.180
and this conversation is taking place

02:35:16.180 --> 02:35:18.100
at low presence penalty.

02:35:18.100 --> 02:35:21.580
So one friend says, hey, give me some good news.

02:35:21.580 --> 02:35:25.220
And the other friend says, Manchester United won six nil.

02:35:25.220 --> 02:35:26.460
It was the best game ever.

02:35:26.460 --> 02:35:29.300
I've never seen Real Madrid fans looking so unhappy.

02:35:29.300 --> 02:35:30.900
Manchester United are the best.

02:35:30.900 --> 02:35:33.740
Let me tell you about the game in detail.

02:35:33.740 --> 02:35:36.540
Hmm, we all know someone like that, right?

02:35:36.540 --> 02:35:38.780
Now, what would happen to this conversation

02:35:38.780 --> 02:35:42.160
if we could flip it to a high presence penalty?

02:35:42.160 --> 02:35:45.220
The first friend says, hey, give me some good news.

02:35:45.220 --> 02:35:48.440
And the reply is, well, my team won on Saturday.

02:35:48.440 --> 02:35:49.940
My investments are doing well.

02:35:49.940 --> 02:35:52.340
My brother's out of hospital, the sun's shining,

02:35:52.340 --> 02:35:54.420
and I'm getting married next June.

02:35:54.420 --> 02:35:56.220
So you can see that instead of obsessing

02:35:56.220 --> 02:35:57.700
over Manchester United,

