03:21:54.420 --> 03:21:56.640
Now We Wing It needs a way to communicate

03:21:56.640 --> 03:21:58.620
with their customers quickly and easily,

03:21:58.620 --> 03:22:00.940
so they want an AI support bot.

03:22:00.940 --> 03:22:04.040
Let's just check what exactly we're trying to achieve here.

03:22:04.040 --> 03:22:07.180
We want a chat bot with the ability to answer questions

03:22:07.180 --> 03:22:09.220
specific to our company.

03:22:09.220 --> 03:22:11.040
If the chat bot doesn't know the answer,

03:22:11.040 --> 03:22:12.680
rather than hallucinate,

03:22:12.680 --> 03:22:15.820
it should advise the user to email or phone.

03:22:15.820 --> 03:22:17.260
Okay, let's crack on.

03:22:17.260 --> 03:22:20.520
Now we know what we're doing and why we're doing it.

03:22:20.520 --> 03:22:23.700
Let's take a high-level overview of the AI process

03:22:23.700 --> 03:22:26.920
to get an idea of how we're going to do it.

03:22:31.020 --> 03:22:34.180
So how do we get this highly tuned chat bot off the ground?

03:22:34.180 --> 03:22:37.020
Let's have a quick look at the whole process.

03:22:37.020 --> 03:22:38.900
Firstly, we need data.

03:22:38.900 --> 03:22:40.800
This is just like the examples we used

03:22:40.800 --> 03:22:42.180
in the few-shot prompts,

03:22:42.180 --> 03:22:45.940
but it's going to be much longer and specifically formatted.

03:22:45.940 --> 03:22:48.940
So we'll be using the CSV format

03:22:48.940 --> 03:22:51.140
or the comma-separated values format.

03:22:51.140 --> 03:22:53.400
It also works in JSON format,

03:22:53.400 --> 03:22:54.740
but basically at this point,

03:22:54.740 --> 03:22:56.340
we need to make sure the chat bot

03:22:56.340 --> 03:22:58.380
has got all of the information it needs

03:22:58.380 --> 03:23:00.100
to do its job properly.

03:23:00.100 --> 03:23:02.460
Now secondly, as the fine-tune process

03:23:02.460 --> 03:23:05.420
takes a long time to run, we don't want errors.

03:23:05.420 --> 03:23:08.620
So let's use OpenAI's data preparation tool

03:23:08.620 --> 03:23:11.500
to process our data so the format is correct

03:23:11.500 --> 03:23:13.380
and it won't get rejected.

03:23:13.380 --> 03:23:16.940
Now this is a CLI or command line interface tool,

03:23:16.940 --> 03:23:19.300
and if you haven't used the command line before,

03:23:19.300 --> 03:23:21.820
don't worry, we'll go through it step by step.

03:23:21.820 --> 03:23:25.600
The third thing we need to do is actually upload our data

03:23:25.600 --> 03:23:29.660
to OpenAI and tell it to make our fine-tuned model.

03:23:29.660 --> 03:23:32.780
Again, we do this with a CLI tool.

03:23:32.780 --> 03:23:34.400
It's very quick to get it going,

03:23:34.400 --> 03:23:36.940
but it can take a long, long time

03:23:36.940 --> 03:23:39.320
for the request to be processed.

03:23:39.320 --> 03:23:41.060
Now when that process ends,

03:23:41.060 --> 03:23:43.800
we will have our own special endpoint,

03:23:43.800 --> 03:23:45.800
which we can then use with our chat bot.

03:23:45.800 --> 03:23:48.300
But at that point, we'll need to think about the changes

03:23:48.300 --> 03:23:50.520
we need to make to our existing code,

03:23:50.520 --> 03:23:51.900
because to use the new model,

03:23:51.900 --> 03:23:54.260
we'll have to make some adjustments.

03:23:54.260 --> 03:23:55.820
All of the code we've got at the moment

03:23:55.820 --> 03:23:59.440
is very specific to the GPT-4 model we've been using.

03:23:59.440 --> 03:24:02.300
We'll have to change things up a little bit here.

03:24:02.300 --> 03:24:04.020
Okay, so that's how it works.

03:24:04.020 --> 03:24:05.660
Let's make a start on the data.

03:24:09.420 --> 03:24:12.420
Okay, so we need some data to fine-tune our model,

03:24:12.420 --> 03:24:15.220
so let's deal with that next.

03:24:15.220 --> 03:24:16.660
And the first question is,

03:24:16.660 --> 03:24:19.460
how much data do you need to fine-tune a model?

03:24:19.460 --> 03:24:23.360
Well, the advice from OpenAI is you should provide

03:24:23.360 --> 03:24:26.280
at least a few hundred high-quality examples

03:24:26.280 --> 03:24:29.120
ideally vetted by human experts.

03:24:29.120 --> 03:24:31.480
And what's more, OpenAI says

03:24:31.480 --> 03:24:33.420
increasing the number of examples

03:24:33.420 --> 03:24:36.260
is usually the best and most reliable way

03:24:36.260 --> 03:24:38.240
of improving performance.

03:24:38.240 --> 03:24:40.620
So if you were setting this up in the real world,

03:24:40.620 --> 03:24:42.680
you would basically grab all of your company's

03:24:42.680 --> 03:24:45.300
support tickets and customer service emails

03:24:45.300 --> 03:24:47.820
and anything else relevant that you can lay your hands on.

03:24:47.820 --> 03:24:49.940
And then as OpenAI recommends,

03:24:49.940 --> 03:24:53.780
you would have a human check it for accuracy and relevance.

03:24:53.780 --> 03:24:55.320
Now for the app we're building today,

03:24:55.320 --> 03:24:58.160
we're going to use a relatively small amount of data,

03:24:58.160 --> 03:24:59.980
but the principle is exactly the same.

03:24:59.980 --> 03:25:01.660
That is what I want to show you,

03:25:01.660 --> 03:25:04.420
the principle of how to fine-tune data.

03:25:04.420 --> 03:25:07.660
Now how we format that data is really important.

03:25:07.660 --> 03:25:11.540
OpenAI wants the data to be in JSON-L format

03:25:11.540 --> 03:25:13.820
and the docs give us an example.

03:25:13.820 --> 03:25:18.020
Now JSON-L is data formatted with JSON on each line.

03:25:18.020 --> 03:25:20.820
Each line has to be valid JSON in its own right

03:25:20.820 --> 03:25:23.640
and each line must end with a new line character.

03:25:23.640 --> 03:25:26.640
Now, if you haven't used JSON-L before, don't worry.

03:25:26.640 --> 03:25:28.680
We won't be writing it ourselves.

03:25:28.680 --> 03:25:31.520
We'll be using a special tool to create it.

03:25:31.520 --> 03:25:34.700
So we'll actually be working with much simpler CSV

03:25:34.700 --> 03:25:36.820
or comma separated values data

03:25:36.820 --> 03:25:40.680
and we'll let OpenAI's tool do the heavy lifting.

03:25:40.680 --> 03:25:43.100
Now as well as wanting JSON-L format,

03:25:43.100 --> 03:25:45.700
OpenAI gives us some further criteria

03:25:45.700 --> 03:25:47.900
for the format of our data.

03:25:47.900 --> 03:25:51.360
It wants each prompt to end with a separator

03:25:51.360 --> 03:25:54.820
to show where the prompt ends and the completion begins.

03:25:54.820 --> 03:25:58.140
It wants each completion to start with a white space

03:25:58.140 --> 03:26:01.540
and it wants each completion to end with a stop sequence

03:26:01.540 --> 03:26:04.660
to inform the model where the completion ends.

03:26:04.660 --> 03:26:06.380
Now the stop sequence is something

03:26:06.380 --> 03:26:09.300
that we haven't needed yet, but we will talk about it

03:26:09.300 --> 03:26:11.180
when we get to that point in the project

03:26:11.180 --> 03:26:13.240
and everything will become clear.

03:26:13.240 --> 03:26:16.160
To be honest, all of this sounds like a bit of a pain

03:26:16.160 --> 03:26:18.280
and actually in their example data,

03:26:18.280 --> 03:26:21.300
they didn't even show us exactly what they wanted

03:26:21.300 --> 03:26:23.920
with the stop sequences and the separator.

03:26:23.920 --> 03:26:26.400
So we don't even have that to help us.

03:26:26.400 --> 03:26:29.380
But good news is right here.

03:26:29.380 --> 03:26:32.620
You can use our CLI data preparation tool

03:26:32.620 --> 03:26:36.220
to easily convert your data into this format.

03:26:36.220 --> 03:26:39.780
So we're going to let that tool do everything for us.

03:26:39.780 --> 03:26:43.020
So don't worry at all if that looks intimidating.

03:26:43.020 --> 03:26:44.380
Okay, in the next grim,

03:26:44.380 --> 03:26:46.880
let's take a look at the data we'll be using.

03:26:50.500 --> 03:26:53.280
Okay, let's take a look at the data we're going to use.

03:26:53.280 --> 03:26:56.060
Now writing JSON out by hand is a pain.

03:26:56.060 --> 03:26:58.540
So I've organized this in a spreadsheet

03:26:58.540 --> 03:27:02.340
using comma separated values or CSV.

03:27:02.340 --> 03:27:04.180
All we've got here is two columns,

03:27:04.180 --> 03:27:06.060
prompt and completion.

03:27:06.060 --> 03:27:08.960
Each prompt is a question from a customer

03:27:08.960 --> 03:27:12.900
and each completion is an answer from customer service.

03:27:12.900 --> 03:27:14.260
So we've just got two columns.

03:27:14.260 --> 03:27:15.860
I think we can work with that.

03:27:15.860 --> 03:27:19.220
And I've got 38 pairs of prompts and completions.

03:27:19.220 --> 03:27:23.500
Ideally, I would have 10 times that number or even more.

03:27:23.500 --> 03:27:25.740
But this small dataset is going to allow us

03:27:25.740 --> 03:27:28.960
to see the principle of how fine tuning works.

03:27:28.960 --> 03:27:33.600
Now I've also pasted the CSV data into this file right here.

03:27:33.600 --> 03:27:35.600
And it doesn't look like it's formatted,

03:27:35.600 --> 03:27:38.680
but actually CSV formatting is really simple.

03:27:38.680 --> 03:27:41.120
We've got the two column headings right here.

03:27:41.120 --> 03:27:44.700
And then each line contains one prompt completion pair.

03:27:44.700 --> 03:27:47.000
So this is the prompt right here

03:27:47.000 --> 03:27:50.460
and this is the completion after the comma.

03:27:50.460 --> 03:27:52.400
Now I just want to draw your attention

03:27:52.400 --> 03:27:55.940
to the last few pairs because here I've done something

03:27:55.940 --> 03:27:58.300
which is just a little bit more complex.

03:27:58.300 --> 03:28:00.040
It's a little bit tricky to see.

03:28:00.040 --> 03:28:03.680
So I'm just going to take this last prompt completion pair

03:28:03.680 --> 03:28:05.540
and space it out a little bit.

03:28:05.540 --> 03:28:09.580
Now, instead of just having one question and one answer,

03:28:09.580 --> 03:28:12.760
the prompt actually consists of several parts.

03:28:12.760 --> 03:28:14.880
The first part is a summary.

03:28:14.880 --> 03:28:17.780
Then we've actually got a short conversation.

03:28:17.780 --> 03:28:21.040
So we've got something that the customer has asked

03:28:21.040 --> 03:28:23.940
and then we've got the agent's reply.

03:28:23.940 --> 03:28:27.100
And then the customer has responded to the agent.

03:28:27.100 --> 03:28:31.620
And then we finish with the agent and a space

03:28:31.620 --> 03:28:35.100
and then we get the completion at the very end.

03:28:35.100 --> 03:28:37.760
So basically all of that is the prompt

03:28:37.760 --> 03:28:40.380
and that is the completion.

03:28:40.380 --> 03:28:42.060
Now I've done that just to show you

03:28:42.060 --> 03:28:44.620
that these prompts don't have to just be one question

03:28:44.620 --> 03:28:45.860
and one answer.

03:28:45.860 --> 03:28:48.480
They can actually involve lots and lots of dialogue.

03:28:48.480 --> 03:28:51.540
So if you have got a lot of customer service data,

03:28:51.540 --> 03:28:54.580
you can format it in this way with the completion

03:28:54.580 --> 03:28:57.300
just being the final answer to the query

03:28:57.300 --> 03:29:00.980
and that is going to really help train your chatbot.

03:29:00.980 --> 03:29:03.700
Now you don't need to stick to one data style.

03:29:03.700 --> 03:29:06.260
As long as you're working with prompts and completions,

03:29:06.260 --> 03:29:08.200
you can mix as I've done here,

03:29:08.200 --> 03:29:10.260
some of the prompts will be one question

03:29:10.260 --> 03:29:14.060
and some of the prompts will actually be whole conversations.

