04:00:11.840 --> 04:00:15.380
but not enough data for a full production ready app.

04:00:15.380 --> 04:00:18.780
But that said, there is one last really important aspect

04:00:18.780 --> 04:00:21.180
of fine tuning that I want to tell you about

04:00:21.180 --> 04:00:24.640
and it is an epochs.

04:00:24.640 --> 04:00:25.780
What does that mean?

04:00:25.780 --> 04:00:27.900
Well, when we were building the model,

04:00:27.900 --> 04:00:31.460
you might have noticed that as the model was fine tuning,

04:00:31.460 --> 04:00:33.900
it completed various epochs.

04:00:33.900 --> 04:00:37.480
And in this case, it completed four epochs.

04:00:37.480 --> 04:00:40.180
Well, maybe four epochs just wasn't enough.

04:00:40.180 --> 04:00:41.460
So in the next grim,

04:00:41.460 --> 04:00:43.360
let's talk about what epochs are,

04:00:43.360 --> 04:00:44.780
how we can get more of them

04:00:44.780 --> 04:00:46.920
and how that is going to help us.

04:00:46.920 --> 04:00:48.940
So let's check that out next.

04:00:52.260 --> 04:00:54.100
Let's talk about an epochs

04:00:54.100 --> 04:00:57.640
and I've put here the number of data cycles.

04:00:57.640 --> 04:01:00.820
So what exactly are an epochs?

04:01:00.820 --> 04:01:02.460
Well, the N stands for number.

04:01:02.460 --> 04:01:04.280
So it's the number of epochs.

04:01:04.280 --> 04:01:07.100
And what that basically means is that it controls

04:01:07.100 --> 04:01:09.420
the number of times open AI will cycle

04:01:09.420 --> 04:01:12.800
through the training dataset when fine tuning the model.

04:01:12.800 --> 04:01:14.260
Now it defaults to four

04:01:14.260 --> 04:01:16.620
and that is likely fine for larger datasets

04:01:16.620 --> 04:01:19.700
with hundreds or thousands of prompt completion pairs.

04:01:19.700 --> 04:01:22.980
But for smaller datasets, it isn't enough.

04:01:22.980 --> 04:01:25.160
The disadvantage of using higher numbers

04:01:25.160 --> 04:01:27.760
and therefore more cycles through the training data

04:01:27.760 --> 04:01:30.140
is that it costs more to do the fine tune

04:01:30.140 --> 04:01:31.960
with a small dataset like ours,

04:01:31.960 --> 04:01:33.660
it's still going to be pretty cheap.

04:01:33.660 --> 04:01:35.620
The other thing to think about is that

04:01:35.620 --> 04:01:37.680
if you're using a massive dataset,

04:01:37.680 --> 04:01:39.900
it will take a long time.

04:01:39.900 --> 04:01:42.180
Now an epochs is something we set

04:01:42.180 --> 04:01:45.660
when we send the training data to be fine tuned.

04:01:45.660 --> 04:01:48.180
We can't set it from within our app.

04:01:48.180 --> 04:01:50.700
So what we need to do is go back to the CLI

04:01:50.700 --> 04:01:51.940
and we're going to add this

04:01:51.940 --> 04:01:54.540
to the end of the fine tuning command.

04:01:54.540 --> 04:01:57.300
It's just dash dash N underscore epochs

04:01:57.300 --> 04:01:59.380
and then the number of epochs you want.

04:01:59.380 --> 04:02:01.420
And I'm going to go for 16.

04:02:01.420 --> 04:02:03.060
You don't have to go for 16,

04:02:03.060 --> 04:02:06.080
but I got much improved results using 16.

04:02:06.080 --> 04:02:07.220
You could go higher,

04:02:07.220 --> 04:02:09.960
but just remember it costs more each time.

04:02:09.960 --> 04:02:12.220
So that is the same command we used before.

04:02:12.220 --> 04:02:14.580
Here's the N epochs added on the end.

04:02:14.580 --> 04:02:16.800
And so the final command will look like this.

04:02:16.800 --> 04:02:18.520
It's everything we did before

04:02:18.520 --> 04:02:22.940
plus this N epochs on the end with the double dash.

04:02:22.940 --> 04:02:25.700
So here is a challenge for you.

04:02:25.700 --> 04:02:29.780
Use open AI CLI tool to build a new fine-tuned model

04:02:29.780 --> 04:02:32.060
with N epochs set to 16.

04:02:32.060 --> 04:02:34.260
You don't need to prepare the data again.

04:02:34.260 --> 04:02:36.460
That command is all you need.

04:02:36.460 --> 04:02:37.980
Once you've gone through that process,

04:02:37.980 --> 04:02:39.780
you will get a new model name

04:02:39.780 --> 04:02:42.420
and you can slide it in right here,

04:02:42.420 --> 04:02:44.420
replacing the old model.

04:02:44.420 --> 04:02:45.260
Once you've done that,

